WEBVTT

NOTE SA1 Topic 9.2: Event Logs. Timings are estimates at 150 words a minute; re-sync after recording.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.600
Welcome to Topic 9.

2
00:00:01.850 --> 00:00:03.050
2, Event Logs.

3
00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:09.700
When something goes wrong on a server, the
event logs are usually the first place to

4
00:00:09.950 --> 00:00:13.550
look, because Windows writes down what
happened and when.

5
00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:19.800
In this topic you will find your way
around Event Viewer and read an event

6
00:00:20.050 --> 00:00:21.250
properly.

7
00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:27.900
You will filter logs, save a custom view
and choose how much history a log keeps.

8
00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:34.150
Then you will ask the same questions in
PowerShell, create test events of your own

9
00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:39.200
with eventcreate, and forward events from
SRV01 to a collector on DC01.

10
00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:43.850
Open Event Viewer and look at the tree on
the left.

11
00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:46.500
Windows Logs holds the classic logs.

12
00:00:46.750 --> 00:00:49.150
Application is for programs and services.

13
00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:52.600
Security holds audit events, like sign-ins
and lockouts.

14
00:00:52.850 --> 00:00:58.050
Setup records roles, features and updates,
and System is Windows itself, its drivers

15
00:00:58.300 --> 00:00:59.500
and services.

16
00:00:59.750 --> 00:01:04.150
Forwarded Events stays empty until this
computer collects events from others.

17
00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:10.800
Under Applications and Services Logs, each
role or app has its own log, such as DNS

18
00:01:11.050 --> 00:01:12.250
Server.

19
00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:18.500
Very detailed Analytic and Debug logs stay
hidden until you show them from the View

20
00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:19.950
menu.

21
00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:22.200
Custom Views are saved filters.

22
00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:26.450
Select an event and the General tab shows
its properties.

23
00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:29.900
The level tells you how serious it is.

24
00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:36.550
The source is the program or component
that wrote the event, and the Event ID is

25
00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:38.000
its number.

26
00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:44.250
Together, source and Event ID name the
kind of event, because the same number can

27
00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:46.900
mean different things from different
sources.

28
00:01:47.150 --> 00:01:52.750
Task Category and Keywords add detail; in
the Security log the keywords say Audit

29
00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:54.600
Success or Audit Failure.

30
00:01:54.850 --> 00:01:58.850
Logged is the time, and Computer is where
it happened.

31
00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:03.500
There are five levels, and PowerShell uses
a number for each.

32
00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:08.950
Critical is one: a serious failure, like a
restart without a clean shutdown.

33
00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:14.400
Error is two: something failed, such as a
service that would not start.

34
00:02:14.650 --> 00:02:19.050
Warning is three: not a failure yet, but
worth a look.

35
00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:23.700
Information is four, and Verbose, five, is
extra detail for debugging.

36
00:02:23.950 --> 00:02:27.950
When people say problems, they mean
Critical, Error and Warning.

37
00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:30.200
The Security log is different.

38
00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:35.650
Success and failure there are keywords,
Audit Success and Audit Failure, so that

39
00:02:35.900 --> 00:02:38.300
log shows a Keywords column first.

40
00:02:38.550 --> 00:02:42.950
A busy log can hold thousands of events,
so you filter.

41
00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:47.600
Select a log, then Filter Current Log in
the Actions pane.

42
00:02:47.850 --> 00:02:50.250
Set Logged to Last 24 hours.

43
00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:52.500
Tick Critical, Error and Warning.

44
00:02:52.750 --> 00:02:58.350
You can stay with the current log or pick
a source, such as SA1Lab.

45
00:02:58.600 --> 00:03:06.200
In the Event IDs box, type a number to
show only that ID, or a minus sign and a

46
00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:08.450
number to leave one out.

47
00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:11.900
Remember, a filter only changes what you
see.

48
00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:16.150
Nothing is deleted, and Clear Filter
brings every event back.

49
00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:21.200
A filter works on one log and disappears
when you clear it.

50
00:03:21.450 --> 00:03:24.650
A custom view is a filter you keep.

51
00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:30.500
Select Create Custom View, tick Critical,
Error and Warning, and choose two logs at

52
00:03:30.750 --> 00:03:32.350
once: Application and System.

53
00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:35.400
Save it with the name SA1 Problems.

54
00:03:35.650 --> 00:03:40.450
It now sits under Custom Views every time
you open Event Viewer.

55
00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:45.500
You can export it and import it on your
other servers too.

56
00:03:45.750 --> 00:03:50.150
Administrative Events is a built-in view
that works the same way.

57
00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:51.600
Four more tools.

58
00:03:51.850 --> 00:03:58.650
Find searches the text of the events in
the current log, which is handy for a name

59
00:03:58.900 --> 00:04:00.100
like SA1Lab.

60
00:04:00.350 --> 00:04:07.550
Save All Events As writes the log to an
evtx file that you can open later on any

61
00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:09.000
computer.

62
00:04:09.250 --> 00:04:14.850
Attach Task To This Event creates a
scheduled task that starts a program each

63
00:04:15.100 --> 00:04:16.700
time that event appears.

64
00:04:16.950 --> 00:04:19.350
We treat it as a concept.

65
00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:22.800
And behind every filter is an XML query.

66
00:04:23.050 --> 00:04:29.850
You can see it on the filter's XML tab,
and the Details tab shows one event as

67
00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:31.300
XML.

68
00:04:31.550 --> 00:04:33.950
Right-click a log and choose Properties.

69
00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:37.800
Maximum log size decides how much history
you keep.

70
00:04:38.050 --> 00:04:42.050
Then you choose what happens when the log
is full.

71
00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:47.100
Overwrite events as needed is the default:
the oldest events go first.

72
00:04:47.350 --> 00:04:54.550
Archive the log when full saves the full
log as a file and starts a new one, so

73
00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:56.400
watch your disk space.

74
00:04:56.650 --> 00:05:02.650
Do not overwrite events sounds safe, but
when the log is full, new events are

75
00:05:02.900 --> 00:05:05.300
simply lost until someone clears it.

76
00:05:05.550 --> 00:05:11.550
One more fact: clearing the Security log
writes event 1102, so a cleared log leaves

77
00:05:11.800 --> 00:05:13.000
a trace.

78
00:05:13.250 --> 00:05:16.050
Get-WinEvent does all of this from
PowerShell.

79
00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:19.900
With ListLog you see each log's size and
mode.

80
00:05:20.150 --> 00:05:22.150
Circular means overwrite as needed.

81
00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:27.200
With LogName and MaxEvents you get the
newest events from one log.

82
00:05:27.450 --> 00:05:29.850
The most useful parameter is
FilterHashtable.

83
00:05:30.100 --> 00:05:33.300
It is Filter Current Log written as code.

84
00:05:33.550 --> 00:05:39.550
Give it the log names, the levels as
numbers, one, two and three for the

85
00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:43.000
problems, and a StartTime, here one day
ago.

86
00:05:43.250 --> 00:05:49.650
You can also use the keys ProviderName for
the source and Id for the Event ID.

87
00:05:49.900 --> 00:05:55.100
To practice, it helps to have an event
whose answer you already know.

88
00:05:55.350 --> 00:05:57.350
eventcreate writes one for you.

89
00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:04.000
In an administrator window, give it the
type with slash T, the ID with slash ID,

90
00:06:04.250 --> 00:06:10.650
the log with slash L, the source with
slash SO and the text with slash D.

91
00:06:10.900 --> 00:06:16.100
Here that is a warning, ID 900, in
Application, from the source SA1Lab.

92
00:06:16.350 --> 00:06:19.950
A new source name is registered the first
time.

93
00:06:20.200 --> 00:06:25.800
Two limits: IDs go from 1 to 1000, and
only Application or System, never

94
00:06:26.050 --> 00:06:27.250
Security.

95
00:06:27.500 --> 00:06:30.300
Event forwarding copies events to one
collector.

96
00:06:30.550 --> 00:06:35.350
In the lab, SRV01 is the source and DC01
is the collector.

97
00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:41.600
They talk over WinRM, which is on by
default, on port 5985, with Kerberos in

98
00:06:41.850 --> 00:06:43.050
the domain.

99
00:06:43.300 --> 00:06:48.500
DC01 runs the Windows Event Collector
service and stores the events in Forwarded

100
00:06:48.750 --> 00:06:49.950
Events.

101
00:06:50.200 --> 00:06:54.600
In a collector-initiated subscription,
DC01 lists its sources and reads their

102
00:06:54.850 --> 00:06:56.850
logs with its computer account.

103
00:06:57.100 --> 00:07:01.500
In a source-initiated subscription, Group
Policy tells the sources where the

104
00:07:01.750 --> 00:07:04.150
collector is, and they push events.

105
00:07:04.400 --> 00:07:06.400
That scales to many servers.

106
00:07:06.650 --> 00:07:11.050
On DC01, open Subscriptions in Event
Viewer and create a subscription.

107
00:07:11.300 --> 00:07:13.300
Name it SA1 SRV01 Warnings.

108
00:07:13.550 --> 00:07:15.950
The destination log is Forwarded Events.

109
00:07:16.200 --> 00:07:20.600
Choose Collector initiated, then Select
Computers, add SRV01 and use Test.

110
00:07:20.850 --> 00:07:26.050
With Select Events, choose the Application
log and the levels Critical, Error and

111
00:07:26.300 --> 00:07:27.500
Warning.

112
00:07:27.750 --> 00:07:31.350
Under Advanced, keep Machine Account and
pick the delivery.

113
00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:36.000
Normal pulls in batches and can take up to
15 minutes.

114
00:07:36.250 --> 00:07:42.250
Minimize Latency pushes within about 30
seconds, which is what you want in a lab.

115
00:07:42.500 --> 00:07:44.900
Here are the command line steps.

116
00:07:45.150 --> 00:07:50.750
On SRV01, add the computer account SA1
backslash DC01 dollar to the local group

117
00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:52.200
Event Log Readers.

118
00:07:52.450 --> 00:07:55.250
Computer accounts end with a dollar sign.

119
00:07:55.500 --> 00:07:57.500
On DC01, run wecutil qc.

120
00:07:57.750 --> 00:08:02.550
It sets the Windows Event Collector
service to a delayed automatic start,

121
00:08:02.800 --> 00:08:06.400
starts it and turns on the Forwarded
Events log.

122
00:08:06.650 --> 00:08:12.250
wecutil gr shows the runtime status of the
subscription for each source; you want

123
00:08:12.500 --> 00:08:13.700
Active.

124
00:08:13.950 --> 00:08:18.350
Last, Get-WinEvent on the ForwardedEvents
log shows your event, and MachineName

125
00:08:18.600 --> 00:08:21.000
tells you it came from SRV01.

126
00:08:21.250 --> 00:08:24.850
You have already used event logs in this
course.

127
00:08:25.100 --> 00:08:26.300
In Lab 6.

128
00:08:26.550 --> 00:08:32.150
3, event 4625 on CLIENT01 recorded each
failed sign-in, and event 4740 on DC01

129
00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:33.600
recorded the lockout.

130
00:08:33.850 --> 00:08:35.050
In Lab 6.

131
00:08:35.300 --> 00:08:40.500
4, event 3076 in the CodeIntegrity
Operational log told you App Control would

132
00:08:40.750 --> 00:08:42.350
have blocked a program.

133
00:08:42.600 --> 00:08:48.600
DHCP writes to its own DHCP-Server logs,
plus the text audit log you read in

134
00:08:48.850 --> 00:08:52.850
Learning Plan 5, and DNS has the DNS
Server log.

135
00:08:53.100 --> 00:08:58.700
The tools from this topic work for all of
them, for example Get-WinEvent with

136
00:08:58.950 --> 00:09:00.950
LogName Security and Id 4740.

137
00:09:01.200 --> 00:09:06.800
Many servers send their events to a
central store, where they are kept longer,

138
00:09:07.050 --> 00:09:09.050
searched and turned into alerts.

139
00:09:09.300 --> 00:09:11.700
Event forwarding is the built-in way.

140
00:09:11.950 --> 00:09:16.750
A SIEM, short for Security Information and
Event Management, collects logs from

141
00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:21.400
servers, firewalls and other devices,
links related events and raises alerts.

142
00:09:21.650 --> 00:09:27.250
Azure Monitor can collect Windows events
with its agent and a data collection rule.

143
00:09:27.500 --> 00:09:33.100
For the Server Plus exam, remember the
idea: size logs, keep copies off the

144
00:09:33.350 --> 00:09:36.550
server, review them and alert on what
matters.

145
00:09:36.800 --> 00:09:38.400
Four mistakes to avoid.

146
00:09:38.650 --> 00:09:41.050
One: searching by Event ID alone.

147
00:09:41.300 --> 00:09:45.700
IDs repeat across sources, so filter by
source and ID together.

148
00:09:45.950 --> 00:09:49.950
Two: clearing a log to make the errors go
away.

149
00:09:50.200 --> 00:09:54.600
The cause is still there, and now the
evidence is gone.

150
00:09:54.850 --> 00:09:57.250
Save first, then fix the cause.

151
00:09:57.500 --> 00:10:00.700
Three: a subscription that fails with
Access denied.

152
00:10:00.950 --> 00:10:05.750
On the source, add the collector's
computer account to Event Log Readers.

153
00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:09.200
Four: waiting for forwarded events with
Normal delivery.

154
00:10:09.450 --> 00:10:14.650
That can take up to 15 minutes; use
Minimize Latency in the lab.

155
00:10:14.900 --> 00:10:16.500
Time to check yourself.

156
00:10:16.750 --> 00:10:18.750
You manage SRV01 and DC01.

157
00:10:19.000 --> 00:10:23.800
First: which FilterHashtable keys show
Error and Warning events from System in

158
00:10:24.050 --> 00:10:25.250
the last day?

159
00:10:25.500 --> 00:10:27.900
Second: eventcreate with ID 1500 fails.

160
00:10:28.150 --> 00:10:29.350
Why?

161
00:10:29.600 --> 00:10:32.800
And can eventcreate write to the Security
log?

162
00:10:33.050 --> 00:10:36.250
Third: DC01's subscription shows Access
denied for SRV01.

163
00:10:36.500 --> 00:10:38.500
What is missing on SRV01?

164
00:10:38.750 --> 00:10:44.750
Fourth: the Application log is set to Do
not overwrite events, and it is full.

165
00:10:45.000 --> 00:10:46.200
What happens next?

166
00:10:46.450 --> 00:10:52.050
Pause the video here and answer all four
before you continue to the answers.

167
00:10:52.300 --> 00:10:53.900
Here are the answers.

168
00:10:54.150 --> 00:10:59.750
One: LogName System, Level two and three,
and StartTime set to Get-Date minus one

169
00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:01.200
day.

170
00:11:01.450 --> 00:11:04.650
Add level one if you want Critical too.

171
00:11:04.900 --> 00:11:10.500
Two: eventcreate accepts IDs from 1 to
1000 only, and it writes only to

172
00:11:10.750 --> 00:11:12.750
Application or System, never Security.

173
00:11:13.000 --> 00:11:18.600
Three: SA1 backslash DC01 dollar is not in
Event Log Readers on SRV01; a

174
00:11:18.850 --> 00:11:21.650
collector-initiated subscription reads
with DC01's computer account.

175
00:11:21.900 --> 00:11:26.700
Four: new events are lost until someone
saves and clears the log.

176
00:11:26.950 --> 00:11:29.750
Overwrite as needed or archiving avoids
that.

177
00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:32.400
Let's see it in the pod.

178
00:11:32.650 --> 00:11:35.050
On SRV01 I open Event Viewer.

179
00:11:35.300 --> 00:11:40.500
In an administrator terminal I run
eventcreate for a warning, ID 900, source

180
00:11:40.750 --> 00:11:41.950
SA1Lab.

181
00:11:42.200 --> 00:11:45.400
After a refresh, there it is in
Application.

182
00:11:45.650 --> 00:11:52.050
I filter for problems in the last 24
hours, then save a custom view called SA1

183
00:11:52.300 --> 00:11:54.300
Problems across Application and System.

184
00:11:54.550 --> 00:11:58.150
Get-WinEvent finds the same event by
source and ID.

185
00:11:58.400 --> 00:12:02.400
Now I add DC01's computer account to Event
Log Readers.

186
00:12:02.650 --> 00:12:07.850
On DC01 I run wecutil qc and create the
subscription with Minimize Latency.

187
00:12:08.100 --> 00:12:14.500
I create one more event on SRV01, and
within a minute it shows up in DC01's

188
00:12:14.750 --> 00:12:17.550
Forwarded Events, with SRV01 as the
computer.

189
00:12:17.800 --> 00:12:19.000
To wrap up.

190
00:12:19.250 --> 00:12:23.650
Events live in Windows Logs and in
Applications and Services Logs.

191
00:12:23.900 --> 00:12:29.900
Source and Event ID name an event, and the
level says how bad it is.

192
00:12:30.150 --> 00:12:35.750
Filters, custom views, and the size and
retention settings help you find and keep

193
00:12:36.000 --> 00:12:37.600
the events that matter.

194
00:12:37.850 --> 00:12:42.250
Get-WinEvent with FilterHashtable asks the
same questions in code, eventcreate gives

195
00:12:42.500 --> 00:12:46.900
you known test events, and forwarding
copies events to a collector.

196
00:12:47.150 --> 00:12:48.350
Lab 9.

197
00:12:48.600 --> 00:12:53.000
2 takes about 75 minutes in NetLab and is
practice only.
